  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nZonal flow has set up over the region with several weak 500 mb \nshortwaves passing through. Without much moisture in place, only\na few isolated sprinkles. Along and north of the NY Thruway, \nthere is just enough moisture for a bit of CAPE to have \ndeveloped downwind of the Great Lakes ahead of an incoming \nshortwave this afternoon. While rain showers will still be \nisolated, a few heavier showers are possible with a low risk of \na lightning strike or two. While most of the area will be dry \nwith breezy and warm afternoon conditions, areas that have \nalready past their leaf peak with dry leaf litter on the ground \nwill have a higher risk of fire spread through Saturday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nModels are coming into better agreement with the remnants of \ntropical storm Isaias moving up into the Ohio River Valley and \nout into the Mid Atlantic. While rainfall is looking likely, \nmodels and ensembles have the 250 mb jet well to the Northwest \nof the moisture plume so a predecessor rain event is very \nunlikely. Still some light to moderate stratiform rain will move\nin late this weekend. Right now, ensembles range from a \nwidespread quarter inch (25th percentile) to 1 inch (75th \npercentile). There is not much of a tail on the high end, with \nthe 95th percentile below 2 inches of rain, though some pockets \nof higher values are in the Catskills and Poconos over 2 inches.\nOnce this remnant is moving off to the east, cold air looks to \nfilter in again.",
